                  "raw": "{\n  \"chapterId\": \"cmwbfsnjt000083lx74dzmw4t\",\n  \"name\": \"1.1 Pure Imaginary Unit $i$\",\n  \"order\": 0,\n  \"content\": \"Since we have known that $x^2 \\\\geq 0$ for every real number $x$, the equation $x^2 = -4$ has no real solution. But if there is a **pure imaginary unit** $i$ such that\\n\\n$$i^2 = -1$$\\n\\nwith acceptance of the usual operations on real numbers and $i$ such as\\n\\n$$(2i)^2 = 4i^2 = 4(-1) = -4 \\\\quad \\\\text{and} \\\\quad (-2i)^2 = 4i^2 = 4(-1) = -4$$\\n\\nthen $x^2 = -4$ has two solutions $2i$ and $-2i$.\\n\\nIf we try to solve $x^2 = -n$, for $n > 0$, like $x^2 = -4$, then $\\\\sqrt{n}i$ and $-\\\\sqrt{n}i$ are solutions because\\n\\n$$(\\\\pm\\\\sqrt{n}i)^2 = ni^2 = n(-1) = -n$$\\n\\n### Worked example 1\\n\\n**Example.** Solve $x^2 + 2x + 5 = 0$.\\n\\n**Solution.**\\n\\n$$x^2 + 2x + 5 = 0$$\\n\\n$$x^2 + 2x + 1 = -4$$\\n\\n$$(x + 1)^2 = -4$$\\n\\n$$x + 1 = \\\\pm\\\\sqrt{4}i$$\\n\\n$$x = -1 \\\\pm 2i$$\\n\\nSo there are two solutions $x = -1 + 2i$ and $x = -1 - 2i$.\\n\\n### Worked example 2\\n\\n**Example.** Solve $x^2 + 2x + 3 = 0$ and check your answers.\\n\\n**Solution.**\\n\\n$$x^2 + 2x + 3 = 0$$\\n\\n$$x^2 + 2x + 1 = -2$$\\n\\n$$(x + 1)^2 = -2$$\\n\\n$$x + 1 = \\\\pm \\\\sqrt{2}i$$\\n\\n$$x = -1 \\\\pm \\\\sqrt{2}i$$\\n\\nSo two solutions are $x = -1 + \\\\sqrt{2}i$ and $x = -1 - \\\\sqrt{2}i$.\\n\\nFor $x = -1 + \\\\sqrt{2}i$,\\n\\n$$\\\\begin{aligned} x^2 + 2x + 3 &= (-1 + \\\\sqrt{2}i)^2 + 2(-1 + \\\\sqrt{2}i) + 3 \\\\\\\\ &= 1 - 2\\\\sqrt{2}i + 2i^2 - 2 + 2\\\\sqrt{2}i + 3 \\\\\\\\ &= 1 - 2\\\\sqrt{2}i - 2 - 2 + 2\\\\sqrt{2}i + 3 \\\\\\\\ &= 0 \\\\end{aligned}$$\\n\\nFor $x = -1 - \\\\sqrt{2}i$,\\n\\n$$\\\\begin{aligned} x^2 + 2x + 3 &= (-1 - \\\\sqrt{2}i)^2 + 2(-1 - \\\\sqrt{2}i) + 3 \\\\\\\\ &= 1 + 2\\\\sqrt{2}i + 2i^2 - 2 - 2\\\\sqrt{2}i + 3 \\\\\\\\ &= 1 + 2\\\\sqrt{2}i - 2 - 2 - 2\\\\sqrt{2}i + 3 \\\\\\\\ &= 0 \\\\end{aligned}$$\"\n}",
                  "options": {
                    "raw": {
                      "language": "json"
                    }
                  }
